---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Src from '../components/Src.astro';
import Aff from '../components/Aff.astro';
const S = {
  ftc: 'https://search.ftc.gov/system/files/ftc_gov/pdf/rental-scams-spotlight-2025.pdf',
  mi: 'https://www.michigan.gov/consumerprotection/protect-yourself/consumer-alerts/scams/rental-listing-scams-how-to-spot-and-dodge-them',
  sc: 'https://consumer.sc.gov/rental-scams',
  smTen: 'https://www.mysmartmove.com/transunion-smartmove-frequently-asked-questions/tenant-frequently-asked-questions',
  ttAcc: 'https://support.turbotenant.com/en/articles/4004041-screening-reports-how-to-access-them-and-resolve-common-issues',
};
const updated = '2026-11-25';
const title = 'Rental Listing Scams: "No Credit Check" Ads, $1 Credit-Check Links and Copied Listings';
const description = 'How rental listing scams work according to the FTC\'s December 2025 data spotlight ($65 million in reported losses since 2020), why a "landlord" asking for your credit score screenshot or a $1 credit check link is a red flag, how real tenant screening works, and what landlords whose listings get copied can do.';
const schema = {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":title,"description":description,"dateModified":updated};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated={updated} schema={schema}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="lede">"No credit check, no background check, move in this week" sounds like a gift when you've been turned down twice. It's also how a lot of rental scams start. Below is what the Federal Trade Commission and two state consumer agencies say about how these scams work, how a real screening request differs, and what to do if your own listing has been copied.</p>

  <div class="note"><strong>The short version.</strong> Since 2020, people have reported nearly 65,000 rental scams to the FTC, with about $65 million in losses <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC Data Spotlight, Dec. 2025" />. Scammers pressure renters to pay before seeing the place, ask for screenshots of credit scores or send links to $1 "credit checks," and collect SSNs, license photos and pay stubs for identity theft <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />. The FTC says "legit landlords normally pull your credit score themselves – they don't tell you to get it for them" <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />.</div>

  <h2>How common are rental listing scams?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Nearly 65,000 reports and about $65 million in reported losses since 2020; the FTC notes most scams are never reported, so this "likely reflects only a fraction of the actual harm" <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />.</li>
    <li>In the 12 months ending June 2025, about half of people who reported a rental scam said it started with a fake ad on Facebook, and 16% said it started on Craigslist <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />.</li>
    <li>People ages 18 to 29 were three times more likely than other adults to report losing money to a rental scam <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />.</li>
    <li>The median reported loss from January 2020 through June 2025 was $1,000 <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC, note 1" />.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What do rental scammers ask for?</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Red flag</th><th>What the agency says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Money before you've seen the place</td><td>An application fee, security deposit or first month's rent to "hold" the property, before you've seen it in person <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Credit score screenshots or a $1 credit check link</td><td>Links to sites offering a credit check "often for $1" that enroll you in a paid membership with recurring fees, with the scammer earning an "affiliate" payment per sign-up <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your SSN, license photo and pay stubs up front</td><td>Collected to steal your identity <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wire transfers, payment apps, gift cards or crypto</td><td>Once sent by wire, "there's no way to get it back"; this includes being asked to use payment apps, gift cards or cryptocurrency <Src href={S.mi} label="Michigan AG" /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Owner is "out of town"</td><td>They can't meet, and offer a key under the mat or someone else to finalize the contract <Src href={S.sc} label="SC Consumer Affairs" /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Self-guided tour code</td><td>Scammers copy listings from landlords who use self-tour services and send a lockbox code to make the listing look legitimate <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Same photos under another name or price</td><td>Search the address; the same property with different prices or contact information is likely a scam, and a for-sale listing is "a big red flag" <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />. South Carolina suggests a reverse image search of the photos <Src href={S.sc} label="SC Consumer Affairs" /></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Is a "no credit check" rental always a scam?</h2>
  <p>No. Some private landlords do skip credit checks. The warning signs are what comes next: pressure to pay before a showing or a lease, an unusual payment method, or a request for your SSN and documents before you've agreed to rent <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" /> <Src href={S.mi} label="Michigan AG" />. Until you've agreed to rent a place, the FTC says, "a landlord doesn't need your Social Security number, credit score or other sensitive information" <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />.</p>

  <h2>How does a real tenant screening request work?</h2>
  <p>On the big screening platforms, you authorize the check yourself, with the screening company, not by sending documents to the landlord:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><Aff id="smartmove" href="https://smartmove.pxf.io/c/6593174/1515180/17713" text="TransUnion SmartMove" /> says the landlord's view doesn't include your Social Security number or account numbers, and the landlord can see your information for 30 days after you approve screening <Src href={S.smTen} label="SmartMove" />.</li>
    <li>TurboTenant says landlords "cannot answer verification questions or authorize screening on their behalf"; the applicant completes identity verification <Src href={S.ttAcc} label="TurboTenant" />.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Some states cap screening fees, and some require the landlord to give you a copy of the report. See <a href="/application-fee-caps-by-state/">application fee caps by state</a> and <a href="/free-tenant-screening-report-copy/">how to get a free copy of your screening report</a>.</p>

  <h2>What can a landlord do when their listing is copied?</h2>
  <p>The FTC says scammers "make it harder for real landlords, many of whom report seeing their listings copied to defraud people" <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />, and that warning signs posted inside homes on self-tour have "helped tip some people off" <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />. Practical steps:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Put your real contact method, application link and fee in every listing, so a copy with different details stands out.</li>
    <li>Post a notice inside self-tour units that you never take payment by wire, gift card, crypto or payment app before a lease.</li>
    <li>Search your address now and then for copies with a different price or contact.</li>
    <li>Report copies to the FTC at <a href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ReportFraud.ftc.gov</a> <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />, and to the site that hosts the fake listing.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where do renters report a rental scam?</h2>
  <p>The FTC takes reports at ReportFraud.ftc.gov and has recovery steps at ftc.gov/scams <Src href={S.ftc} label="FTC" />. State consumer agencies, such as the <a href={S.mi} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michigan Attorney General</a> and the <a href={S.sc} target="_blank" rel="noopener">South Carolina Department of Consumer Affairs</a>, also take complaints.</p>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/verify-applicant-employer-business-records/">Checking an applicant's employer in state business records</a></li>
    <li><a href="/how-long-tenant-screening-takes/">How long tenant screening takes</a></li>
    <li><a href="/tenant-screening-without-ssn-itin/">Screening applicants without an SSN</a></li>
    <li><a href="/holding-deposits-and-admin-fees-by-state/">Holding deposits and admin fees by state</a></li>
    <li><a href="/">All Screening Atlas guides</a></li>
  </ul>
</Base>
